# hello world
#
# The world, with the third direction put in.
#
# Every other copy is flat and gets by on turning. This one is a solid: nine
# hundred and twenty-six corners, forty-four to the round and twenty-two from
# pole to pole, which are the numbers it has always had. The same waves decide
# where the land is. Anything that opens a model will open it, and a printer
# of the newer kind, the kind that works in layers, will make it.
#
# In a file of this sort a line beginning with a hash is a remark and is
# skipped by the machine. In this world a hash is high ground in full sun.
# You are therefore reading the only mountains in the file that the machine
# will not print. The others follow, as numbers.
#
# One unit is from the middle of the world to the sea. Scale to taste; at
# forty millimetres to the unit it comes out the size of an orange. Up is
# north, as carried. The side facing you when it opens is the side that
# faces you on the page at the moment you arrive, before it has turned at all.
#
# There is no light in the file. Supply your own, from the upper left.

# The corners. North first, then round and down, a ring at a time.
o world
